theorem a-divisor-negating-a-quotient
  note        algebra divides by −(x/2), a quotient under a negation, from a
              line saying −(x/2) is not zero: x/2 is not zero because its
              negation is not.
  let x ∈ ℝ                                                           (H1)
  assume −(x/2) ≠ 0                                                   (H2)
  then x/(−(x/2)) = −2

1.  x/(−(x/2)) = −2
    algebra
    requires −(x/2) ≠ 0: from H2

theorem an-absolute-value-over-its-negation
  note        A cited equation, and a divisor −(p/2): the claim's sides are
              multiplied by −p, which is not zero because −(p/2) is not.
  let p ∈ ℝ                                                           (H1)
  assume |p/2| = p/2                                                  (H2)
  assume |−(p/2)| = |p/2|                                             (H3)
  assume −(p/2) ≠ 0                                                   (H4)
  then |−(p/2)|/(−(p/2)) = −1

1.  |−(p/2)|/(−(p/2)) = −1
    algebra, from H2, H3
    requires −(p/2) ≠ 0: from H4
